Q. The recently created Nginx pod status is pending. Find out, why?
ANS : 
When a pod is stuck in Pending, it means Kubernetes hasn’t been able to schedule it on any node yet.
| Problem                          | Typical Error in Events                           |
| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| No available resources (CPU/mem) | `0/3 nodes are available: Insufficient cpu.`      |
| Node taints                      | `No nodes available to schedule pods.`            |
| PVC not bound                    | `pod has unbound PersistentVolumeClaims`          |
| Wrong nodeSelector               | `node(s) didn't match node selector`              |
| Affinity constraints             | `node(s) didn't match pod affinity/anti-affinity` |
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
✅ Quick Checklist to fix:

If it’s resource shortage: scale your nodes up.
If it’s taint: add toleration or remove taint.
If PVC is pending: create matching PersistentVolume.
If nodeSelector is wrong: adjust labels or selectors.

Here’s a step-by-step process you can follow to find the root cause:
🔹 1️⃣ . Describe the Pod
This is always the first command you should run:

      - > kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
✅ Check carefully under:

Events: This section will usually tell you exactly why it’s Pending.
Common messages you might see:
0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient cpu.
0/3 nodes are available: 3 node(s) had taint {key=value: NoSchedule}
persistentvolumeclaim is not bound
-------------------------------------
🔹 2️⃣ Check Node Resources
If there are no scheduling errors in events, it might be resource exhaustion:

✅ List nodes and see available resources:

        - > kubectl describe nodes or kubectl get nodes -o wide

✅ Look for:

Allocatable CPU / memory
Disk pressure
Memory pressure
Node conditions (Ready, NotReady)
------------------------------------------------------
🔹 3️⃣ Check for Taints
Nodes may have taints preventing scheduling:
    -> kubectl describe nodes | grep -A5 Taints
Taints: node-role.kubernetes.io/master:NoSchedule
This means only pods with proper tolerances can run there.
------------------------------------------------------
🔹 4️⃣ Check PersistentVolume Claims
If your pod uses a PVC, make sure it is Bound:
   - > kubectl get pvc
✅ If you see Pending here, the pod will also stay Pending.
-------------------------------------
🔹 5️⃣ Check Pod Affinity / Anti-Affinity Rules
Your pod spec might have scheduling rules that can’t be satisfied.
✅ Review your pod YAML:

kubectl get pod <pod-name> -o yaml
Look under:
affinity
nodeSelector
tolerations
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Q. Previously created Nginx webserver deployed successfully, but the web page is not accessible, find out, why?
ANS : 
| Cause                                   | What to Check                     |
| --------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Nginx not listening on port 80          | Pod logs, netstat inside pod      |
| Service misconfigured                   | Wrong selector or targetPort      |
| Port blocked by firewall/security group | Cloud firewall rules              |
| NodePort not exposed                    | Confirm NodePort value and access |
| NetworkPolicy blocking                  | Check network policies            |
| LoadBalancer pending                    | EXTERNAL-IP assigned              |
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
kubectl get pods – confirm Running
kubectl logs – look for Nginx errors
kubectl describe svc – validate Service
Test internally with busybox
Confirm external access (NodePort or LoadBalancer)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
